    <!-- Unit Overview -->
    <section class="welcome-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-compass section-icon"></i> Unit Overview</h2>
        <p>The core concept is the shift from "doing" (functions) to "being" (objects). This unit introduces OOP as a paradigm that standardises both behaviour and data structure. Key focus is on the Modularity achieved by custom data types (Classes) and the Abstraction of hiding internal storage mechanisms behind public interfaces.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Syllabus Links -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-bookmark section-icon"></i> What You'll Learn</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.1.1</strong> — Evaluation of OOP fundamentals: standardising data structure and behaviour.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.1.2</strong> — UML Class diagrams: Class names, attributes (fields), and method signatures with visibility indicators (+/-).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.1.4</strong> — Python implementation: class definition, the __init__(self) constructor, and object instantiation.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.1.5</strong> — Encapsulation: Information hiding, access modifiers (public vs. private naming conventions in Python), and the use of accessors (getters) and mutators (setters).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>B3.1.3</strong> — Static vs. Non-static: Understanding class-level variables (shared) versus instance-level variables (unique).
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

