    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Procedural Habits:</strong> Students often try to write logic outside the class or pass every variable as a parameter rather than using self.</li>
            <li><strong>Private Variables in Python:</strong> Believing __variable is "secure" encryption rather than a naming convention (name mangling) meant to prevent conflicts.</li>
            <li><strong>Static vs. Instance:</strong> Confusion over why changing a static variable affects all objects (e.g., changing a global interest rate vs. an individual account balance).</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Structured Knowledge:</strong> Is the highly structured nature of OOP less open to interpretation than procedural programming? Does categorisation limit how we can "know" an object?</li>
                <li><strong>Naming as Knowledge:</strong> "The map is not the territory." Does knowing the name of a class attribute reflect our knowledge of the actual data?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
